===Etymology 2===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*h₂eḱ-}}
[[Image:Wheat close-up.JPG|thumb|right|'''Ears''' of wheat.]]
{{anchor|earofcorn}}<!--linked-to from [[w:Corn on the cob]]-->
From {{inh|en|enm|eere}}, {{m|enm|er}}, from {{inh|en|ang|ēar}} ([[Northumbrian]] dialect {{m|ang|æhher}}), from {{inh|en|gmw-pro|*ahaʀ}}, from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*ahaz}}, from {{inh|en|ine-pro|*h₂eḱ-||sharp}}.

See also {{cog|fy|ier}}, {{cog|nl|aar}}, {{cog|de|Ähre}}; also {{cog|la|acus||needle; husk}}, {{cog|txb|āk||ear, awn}}, {{cog|cu|ость||wheat spike, sharp point}}. More at {{m|en|edge}}.

The sense for an ear of a loaf of bread comes via the French {{l|fr|épi}}.<ref name="epi">{{cite-book |en |text=Epi means an ''''ear''' of wheat' and the idea is that everyone can pull off their own ''''ear'''' from the loaf. The epi is made in the same way as the baguette but, instead of slashing the top, the '''ears''' are formed with scissor cuts. |title=Crumb: Show the Dough Who's Boss |year=2019-02-07 |last=Bertinet |first=Richard |isbn=9780857835727 |publisher=Octopus}}</ref>

